A||N||7||FieroSTETZ||need help-||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||06-18-2000||09:09 PM||||when i was installing the new water pump, there was a little rubber hose coming off of a cylinder that sits next to the intake manifold (passenger side) and the tube turned out to be 5 inches long, with the end all burned up... now, i am getting an EGR message, a code 32- should reattaching this hose make the check engine light go away? where on earth does the other end of this tube go?||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||06-19-2000||12:10 AM||||Is this car a V6?  Was the little cylinder black, with an electrical connector and about 3 vacuum hoses going to it?||||reg||3
Z||000002||StuGood||06-19-2000||01:06 AM||||...and a little short black tube that comes out of one spot on the cylinder, then turns around and re-enters the cylinder at another location?  ||||reg||5
Z||000003||FieroSTETZ||06-19-2000||01:17 AM||||thats it! is that the "egr solenoid"? if so, where does the other end go?||||reg||10
Z||000004||StuGood||06-19-2000||01:30 AM||||I have no idea what you're talking about  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Yes - it's the EGR solenoid.  From your description, I think the hose you're referring to (not the little vinyl ones, right?) is the one by itself, on the front end of the cylinder.  That one goes to a metal tube on the firewall, pretty much directly forward of where we're talking about, maybe down a little.  Near the A/C pulley.  If you trace the metal tube, you'll see it eventually goes to the air cleaner.<P>You might find the other part of the hose still attached to the metal line, hanging down.<P>For what it's worth, the other 2 (little vinyl) tubes go to EGR valve & intake plenum.<P>   ||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroSTETZ||06-19-2000||10:39 PM||||could this rubber hose being detatched cause me to get a code 32?||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||06-19-2000||10:55 PM||||Not sure.  Try it and see.  It won't cost much to get a new piece of hose and hook it up the way it was meant to be.  You'll need about 14".  Be sure to erase ECM trouble codes by disconnecting the battery, etc. <P>My guess is, if the "all burned up" end was melted shut, it wouldn't be good. <P>If memory serves, the metal tube on the firewall provides a path to the "clean" side of the air filter.  So if that tube is sitting there unconnected, it'll be sucking in raw dust and crud. Your engine won't like that    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">!!<BR> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-19-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000007||ponya||06-25-2000||12:57 AM||||Yes, that will set a code 32.  Any vacuum loss to that EGR solenoid will set that code.  Check all hoses, rubber and hard plastic (running underneath the plenum), then disconnect batt (to erase code) then give it a shot.  There is a vacuum sensor in that solenoid (the little hose that seems to loop right back into the solenoid).  If there's no vac, you'll get that code.  Good luck.||||reg||1
